Negative control — the READ path's SCOPE SEAM (AC-2), the story's headline claim.

Hardwires the SESSION's own client into the two READ urls — the collection's
list request (`loadList`) and the manager's per-address read (`loadOne`) — by
re-reading `useActiveSession().useContext().activeUser` inside each function
instead of addressing the `clientId` ref the scope resolved.

The seam is left standing. `resolveClientId` still exists, still branches on
`.for('client', id)`, still feeds `queryKey`, `isAddressable`, `enabled`, and
every mutation. Only the two read urls stop consulting it. That is the FE-2824
shape stated exactly: the structure is all present, the capability is gone.

This is the pre-conversion defect restored verbatim. On `main` the module's
`loadList` read `const { clientId } = useSession()` in its own body
(`modules/client/address/services.ts:33-34`) and every other request function
did the same — `parity.yaml` W12/L10, the module's live FE-2824. A request that
addresses the session's client instead of the scope's is precisely the failure
AC-2 exists to detect.

Declares AC-2 — "the collection addresses the scope's client, not the session's
by accident": the captured request url's `{clientId}` segment must equal the
SCOPE-resolved client id.

Should flip RED — any AC-2 read-back in which the scope-resolved client and the
session's `activeUser` are DIFFERENT ids.

MEASURED, and stated plainly: against the suite at `67f8be413` this mutant
flips NOTHING — 112 passed / 1 failed, identical to the unmutated baseline (the
one failure is AC-20 / finding F6, escalated per `review-notes.md` §7.6, red
with or without it). That is the finding, not a defect in the mutant. With a
single live actor whose session client IS the scope-resolved client, "read the
session" and "read the seam" resolve to the same id, so a url assertion passes
either way.

It is also why the EARLIER AC-2 control — which deleted the `.for('client', id)`
branch out of `resolveClientId` altogether — flipped nothing and had to be
withdrawn; see `client-address.scope-identity.must-fail.patch`, which attacks
the PIN instead and therefore declares AC-30, not AC-2.

The discriminator AC-2 needs is a setup in which the scope-resolved client and
the session's `activeUser` are different ids. That belongs to the test layer and
is authored independently of this file. Until it lands, AC-2's control is
present and honest but not yet demonstrated red.

What this deliberately does NOT touch:
  - The addressability guard. `isAddressable(clientId.value)` keeps reading the
    seam in both functions, so AC-3 / AC-11 / AC-13's empty-capture-log
    read-backs stay green and a red here can never be mistaken for an auth
    regression.
  - `queryKey`. Still scope-keyed, so a red localises to the URL — the thing
    AC-2 asserts — and not to cache partitioning.
  - The WRITE path. `add` / `update` / `remove` / `setDefault` still address the
    seam. The mutation is read-only by construction, which is what makes it
    AC-2's control rather than a second AC-30 control.

Compiles clean under the mutation (`pnpm --filter @upmind-automation/headless
type-check`, exit 0) — a control that cannot build proves nothing.

